  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>To reach this beach, first park at the Parking des Terrasses, right next to the Port Grimaud tourist office (paid parking from April to October), go through the Port Grimaud Sud barriers, follow the road to the right, then continue straight ahead for about 10 minutes (Grimaud tourist office). It is not wheelchair accessible.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>As on all eight beaches of the town of Grimaud, access is regulated by a municipal bylaw, cited by the Grimaud tourist office (we have not read the text of the bylaw itself): from mid-September to the end of May, dogs are accepted all day without restriction; from 1 June to 15 September, they are welcome from 9pm to 6am, and if they swim, it must be outside the marked bathing zones (15 June to 15 September). A leash is requested on the beaches, and remember waste bags.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: bring water for your dog and something to pick up his waste for the roughly 10-minute walk from the car park: the tourist office lists no shower or toilets on this beach.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The lake-city of Port Grimaud is very close. It is also the starting point of the <a href="/fiche-balade-sentier-littoral-grimaud">coastal path to Saint-Tropez or Sainte-Maxime</a>.</p>
    </div>

